course: politics
lesson: 10
title: The news: who makes it and how
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The news is made by people: reporters find out what happened by going to the place and talking to people, and editors decide which stories go in, which go first and which are left out, because there is never room for everything.
explain: Think of your class newspaper with ten stories and room for four: whoever chooses is shaping what everyone thinks matters, and grown-up news works the same way.
explain: A news report tries to say what happened; an opinion says what someone thinks about it; both can be on the same channel, so ask yourself which one you are hearing.
explain: First reports, especially "breaking news", are often unclear or wrong, and headlines are short and made to grab you, so read past the headline, check more than one source, and notice who is speaking: a named spokesperson or an unnamed "source close to the team".
explain: News shows what is unusual, so it often feels sad: nobody reports the planes that land safely; and a good news source owns its mistakes by printing corrections, which is a sign you can trust it more, not less.
fact: A reporter finds out what happened and tells the public; a live report is shown as it happens, often from the place where the story is.
fact: Breaking news is news about something happening right now; early reports are often unclear or wrong, and good news channels correct themselves as facts come in.
fact: A headline is the big title of a story; it is short and made to grab attention, so it can make things sound bigger or simpler than the full story; clickbait is a headline made mainly to get clicks.
fact: An editor decides which stories are used, which go first and how long they are, and checks them; those choices shape what people think is important.
fact: A news report tells what happened; an opinion piece or commentator says what someone thinks about it.
fact: News mostly covers things that are new, surprising or important, which often means problems; millions of good, ordinary things happen every day but are not news.
fact: Different news channels choose different stories, words and experts, so the same event can sound very different; comparing several sources shows what they agree on.
fact: Bias means leaning towards one side; a source is where information comes from.
fact: Some news programmes are made for children, such as Newsround in the UK and Behind the News in Australia; they cover real news but explain words and background.
fact: A spokesperson speaks officially for a group; an anonymous source gives information but does not want their name used, so reporters should check what they say carefully.
fact: A correction is a notice fixing a mistake in a news story; admitting mistakes shows a news source cares about accuracy.
fact: People argue about anonymous sources: some say they help reveal truths people are scared to share, others say we cannot judge information if we do not know who said it.
exercise: Sam sees a reporter standing in the rain outside a building. Why doesn't she go inside? | Reporting from the place helps viewers feel close to the story | She is not allowed into any building at all, by law, while she is filming a report | She forgot her keys and is locked out | Rain makes the microphone work better
exercise: Liam sees BREAKING NEWS, then the details change. Why? | Early reports are often unclear, and they change as facts come in | The channel likes to trick its viewers on purpose, to keep them watching all evening | Breaking news is always made up | The reporter forgot what she said
exercise: Zara reads "Chocolate makes kids smarter!" but one small study found a tiny link. What is the lesson? | Read the whole story, not just the headline | Headlines are always the most accurate part | Eat more chocolate before every single test | Small studies always prove very big things
exercise: What is clickbait? | A headline made mainly to get clicks | Bait used for fishing near a computer | A correction printed at the bottom of a page | A news show made for children on weekdays
exercise: Ruby's school paper has ten stories but room for four. Who decides? | The editor | The reporter in the rain | The readers, by voting first | The printer machine
exercise: Why do editors' choices matter? | They shape what people think is important | They decide who wins elections | They change what really happened | They only choose the colour of the paper
exercise: A man says, "In my opinion, this is the worst idea ever." Another reads out the facts calmly. Which is the news report? | The calm person reading out what happened | The man saying what he thinks | Both of them are exactly the same | Neither, because news has no facts
exercise: What is a commentator? | A person paid to give their opinion | A person who counts all the votes on TV | A person who reads only the weather news | A person who prints all the corrections
exercise: Hannah asks why the news is always sad. What does Grandad mean by "Nobody reports the planes that land safely"? | News shows what is unusual, not everything that happens | Planes never land safely anywhere, so there is nothing good to report about them | Reporters are not allowed on planes | Good news is against the law to report
exercise: Mateo's mum and dad watch the same story on two channels and it sounds different. Why? | Channels choose different words, experts and parts to show | One of the channels is reporting about a different country with the same name | Stories change if you watch them at night | Only one of the channels has reporters
exercise: What is the best way to see the whole picture of a big story? | Compare two or three different sources | Watch only your favourite channel | Read only the headlines quickly | Believe the first thing you hear
exercise: Priya watches Newsround. What is it? | A real news programme made for children | A cartoon that makes up silly stories | A game show about the news | An advert for a newspaper
exercise: Jake hears "a government spokesperson told us". Who is that? | Someone who speaks officially for a group | A person whose name is always secret | A reporter who works for the government | A judge who checks the news
exercise: What is an anonymous source? | Someone who gives information but does not want their name used | Someone who speaks officially for the government on TV, with their name shown on screen | A newspaper with no name on the front | A reporter who never leaves the office
exercise: Dylan sees "Correction: the bridge cost $3 million, not $5 million." What does it show? | The paper cares about getting facts right | The paper should never be trusted again by anyone | The bridge has got cheaper since yesterday | The paper was lying on purpose and got caught
exercise: What does bias mean? | Leaning towards one side instead of being fair | Telling only the facts with no opinion | Counting the votes very slowly | Reporting the news in two languages
test: Your mate goes, "Breaking news said 50 people, then 30. So the news is all cap." ("Cap" means lies.) What is the best reply? | Early reports change as facts come in; good channels update them | He's right, the news is always lies, so you should never watch any of it ever again | The first number is always the true one | The news changed the number to scare us
test: Your little sister reads "Chocolate makes kids SMARTER!" and goes, "Bussin', let's eat loads before the test." What should you tell her? | The headline stretches one small study; scientists said more tests are needed | She's right, chocolate is proven to make you clever, so the more she eats the better she'll do | Chocolate makes you less clever, the headline is backwards | Headlines are always tested by scientists
test: A boomer neighbour says, "In my day the news was just facts, none of this opinion jazz." Which is true today? | News and opinion can be on the same channel, so it helps to ask which you are hearing | Opinion is never allowed on the news by law, so everything a news channel says is plain fact | The news today is only opinion | Facts were only invented recently
test: A Liverpool lad says, "That paper printed a correction, so they're dead dodgy, la." ("Dead dodgy" means really untrustworthy.) What's the fairer view? | Printing corrections shows they care about accuracy | He's right, any single mistake means you should never trust them | Corrections mean the paper made the whole story up from nothing | Papers that print corrections should be closed down
test: What would happen if every channel only showed bad news and never mentioned ordinary good things? | People might think the world is more dangerous than it is | People would know exactly how often bad things happen in the world, day by day | Bad things would stop happening | Good things would stop happening too
test: In the car, your dad says, "Only this channel tells the truth, the rest are biased, end of." Which is the most sensible reply? | Every source can lean a bit, so comparing a few helps | He's right, one channel is always perfect and never wrong at all | All channels are lying to everybody all of the time | Never watch any news at all, from any channel, ever again
test: A teen says, "A source close to the team said it, so it's 100% legit." ("Legit" means true.) What is the careful view? | Unnamed sources can be important, but we can't judge them easily, so reporters must check | Anything from a source is always true | Unnamed sources are always lying, which is exactly why reporters print them without any names | Only sources with funny names can be trusted
test: A Scottish gran says, "Turn it aff, hen, it's aw greetin' and misery." ("Hen" means dear; "greetin'" means crying.) Why does the news seem sad? | News reports what is unusual, which is often problems | The news is made only by sad people who want everyone else to feel sad too | Only sad things happen in Scotland | Happy news is banned from TV
test: In a cartoon, a reporter makes up a story and it goes straight on TV with nobody checking. What should be put right? | Editors check stories and choose what goes in | Reporters can say anything with no checking | The King checks every news story | Viewers write all the news themselves
test: Your friend, being formal, says, "Kindly distinguish between the report and the editorial." What is she asking? | Tell apart what happened from what someone thinks about it | Count how many words each story has and say which one is the longest | Find which story has the biggest picture | Say which channel has the best presenter
test: Your cousin says, "Kids' news like Behind the News is baby stuff, it's not real news." What's true? | It covers real news but explains words and background | It is made-up stories for little children, read out like a bedtime book | It only reports the news about cartoons and toys | It is really just the same thing as an advert for a channel
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of an editor? | The selection, ordering and verification of the stories to be published | The gathering of information exclusively at the scene of events, without any subsequent checking | The expression of personal opinion upon each item of news | The counting of votes on behalf of a broadcaster
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning initial reports of breaking news. | Such reports are frequently incomplete or inaccurate and are revised as facts emerge | Such reports are invariably accurate in every particular and are never subsequently revised | Such reports are fabricated deliberately by broadcasters | Such reports are prohibited from subsequent amendment
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with sound reasoning for an individual wishing to ascertain the facts of a contested event. | Comparison of the accounts given by several different sources | Exclusive reliance upon a single preferred broadcaster | Exclusive reliance upon the headline of a single article | Acceptance of the first account encountered
exam: It is given that a newspaper has published a correction of an earlier error. Determine the inference most consistent with sound reasoning. | The newspaper demonstrates a concern for accuracy | The newspaper is wholly unworthy of any further trust | The newspaper invented the original story deliberately | The newspaper is obliged by law to cease publication
